<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Rui</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 640 BCE (approx.) · capital Rui</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Ji-clan state on the Yellow River bend whose rich cemetery at Liangdai and outspoken minister Rui Liangfu make it one of the better-known minor Zhou states.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Rui was a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou</a> fief held by a branch of the royal Ji clan, its lords ranked as earls. It lay along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> where it divides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruicheng">Ruicheng</a> and later at Liangdai village by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hancheng">Hancheng</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Earl Rui Liangfu served at the royal court and is remembered for warning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-li-of-zhou">King Li of Zhou</a> against monopolising the kingdom's resources. In 709 BCE Earl Wan of Rui was driven out by his mother, Rui Jiang, and the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and Jin intervened. The Liangdai cemetery, excavated from 2005, yielded fine jades and gold ornaments.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Wedged between rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and Jin, Rui was annexed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> in 640 BCE as Qin pushed east towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1046 BCE – 640 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:38.89%;width:8.08%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 640 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rui</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Earl Wan of Rui</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rui</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-843&amp;focus=state-of-rui" aria-label="State of Rui on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6691 1445 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Rui lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7011 1701L7768 1187"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7011 1701h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7011 1701h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rui. <a href="/atlas?year=-843&amp;focus=state-of-rui">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

